; `strict` inside `restrict(...)`: an unsettleable claim is REFUSED
; (AX3057) instead of warned (AX3051).
;
; `391-restrict-effect-transparent.ax` is this file's other half. It
; records why the default is a warning: a body that calls one of its
; own parameters is a correct program the walk cannot follow, and
; refusing it would make dispatch through a stored function
; unwriteable under any restriction rather than merely unverified.
; `severity.policy` states that decision and this file does not
; reverse it.
;
; WHAT `strict` ADDS IS THE AUTHOR'S SIDE OF THAT ARGUMENT. A
; restriction on a sensitive operation is asked for as a GUARANTEE, and
; a guarantee the compiler could not check is not one. A reader who
; sees `restrict(no-io)` and assumes it was verified is worse off than
; a reader who sees nothing at all, which is the whole reason an
; unproven claim can be worth less than no claim. `strict` says: prove
; it or refuse the program.
;
; `strict` IS A MODIFIER AND NOT A RESTRICTION. It names nothing a body
; must not do; it says what happens when the walk cannot settle what
; the body does. So it is neither dispatched as a restriction nor
; reported as an unknown name - `strictOnly` below carries it with no
; restriction beside it and is silent, where any other unknown word in
; that list is AX3052.
;
;   `hardIo`      no-io, strict        -> AX3057: calls a parameter
;   `hardAlloc`   no-alloc, strict     -> AX3057: same, other row
;   `hardRec`     no-recursion, strict -> AX3057: the cycle a parameter
;                                          edge hides is exactly what
;                                          a guarantee has to refuse
;
;   `softIo`      no-io                -> AX3051, a warning, unchanged:
;                                          the default is not touched
;   `provable`    no-io, strict        -> SILENT. The walk settles it
;                                          and it holds, which is what
;                                          `strict` is asking for -
;                                          this arm is the one that
;                                          fails if `strict` starts
;                                          refusing everything.
;   `refuted`     no-io, strict        -> AX3049 and NOT AX3057. The
;                                          body performs IO concretely,
;                                          so the claim is REFUTED, and
;                                          refuted beats unproven the
;                                          same way it beats
;                                          unverifiable in 391.
;   `strictOnly`  strict               -> SILENT, and specifically not
;                                          AX3052: a modifier alone
;                                          restricts nothing.
;   `lexStrict`   no-cast, strict      -> SILENT: `no-cast` is lexical
;                                          and needs no call resolved,
;                                          so there is nothing for
;                                          `strict` to escalate.
(import IO)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io, strict)
(:: hardIo (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (hardIo f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc, strict)
(:: hardAlloc (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (hardAlloc f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-recursion, strict)
(:: hardRec (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (hardRec f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: softIo (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (softIo f n) (f n))

;@axiom:restrict(no-io, strict)
(:: provable (-> Int Int))

(fn (provable n) (+ n 1))

;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:restrict(no-io, strict)
(:: refuted (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (refuted n) { (println "loud") n })

;@axiom:restrict(strict)
(:: strictOnly (-> Int Int))

(fn (strictOnly n) n)

;@axiom:restrict(no-cast, strict)
(:: lexStrict (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))

(fn (lexStrict f n) (f n))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (hardIo provable 1)
    (hardAlloc provable 1)
    (hardRec provable 1)
    (softIo provable 1)
    (provable 1)
    (refuted 1)
    (strictOnly 1)
    (lexStrict provable 1)
  }
)
